C = [
("h1", "11. Galatians 5:16–26: The Flesh, the Spirit and the Limits Within Them"),
("p", "Claim: Galatians 5:16–26 is read here for the limits it sets and the commands it gives. It names works of the flesh with a warning, so that no liberty is given there, and it names the fruit of the Spirit, “against such there is no law.” It does not lay down a rule for each open matter. The walk in the Spirit is read in Study 44, and this chapter does not restate it."),
("sub", "11.1 Walk in the Spirit"),
("p", "“This I say then, Walk in the Spirit, and ye shall not fulfil the lust of the flesh” (Galatians 5:16). The sentence is a command with a promise. The verse that follows states the conflict. “For the flesh lusteth against the Spirit, and the Spirit against the flesh: and these are contrary the one to the other: so that ye cannot do the things that ye would” (Galatians 5:17). Then: “But if ye be led of the Spirit, ye are not under the law” (Galatians 5:18). The volume reports the three verses together as Paul sets them, and it takes from them only what bears on liberty. The believers are commanded to walk in the Spirit. The flesh and the Spirit are opposed. Those led of the Spirit are not under the law. What the walk is, how the Spirit leads and how the verse bears on the Law’s jurisdiction are read in Studies 44 and 59."),
("sub", "11.2 The works of the flesh: no liberty"),
("p", "“Now the works of the flesh are manifest, which are these; Adultery, fornication, uncleanness, lasciviousness, idolatry, witchcraft, hatred, variance, emulations, wrath, strife, seditions, heresies, envyings, murders, drunkenness, revellings, and such like: of the which I tell you before, as I have also told you in time past, that they which do such things shall not inherit the kingdom of God” (Galatians 5:19–21). The list names sins of the body (adultery, fornication, uncleanness, lasciviousness, drunkenness, revellings), of religion (idolatry, witchcraft), and of the temper and the party (hatred, variance, emulations, wrath, strife, seditions, heresies, envyings, murders). It ends with “and such like,” so it is a sample, not a full code."),
("p", "The warning, “shall not inherit the kingdom of God,” is reported as written. The volume does not say whose kingdom is meant or what the warning means for a believer who falls, since Paul does not say in this verse. It reads the list for what it does in the argument: it shows what the liberty of verse 13 is not. A believer who treats these as matters of liberty has used liberty “for an occasion to the flesh.” Drunkenness is on the list as a work of the flesh, and Romans 14:21 names wine as a thing a brother may stumble over. The two are not the same sentence. One names drunkenness, which is forbidden; the other names the drinking of wine, which is not forbidden but may be given up. The volume keeps them apart."),
("sub", "11.3 The fruit of the Spirit"),
("p", "“But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, longsuffering, gentleness, goodness, faith, meekness, temperance: against such there is no law” (Galatians 5:22, 23). The list is of dispositions, not acts. It carries no “and such like,” and it is given as the contrast to the works of the flesh. “Against such there is no law” says that no law stands against these things. It does not say that law has nothing to do with the believer. The volume reports the verse as written and takes it as the positive end of the walk."),
("p", "“And they that are Christ’s have crucified the flesh with the affections and lusts” (Galatians 5:24). “If we live in the Spirit, let us also walk in the Spirit” (Galatians 5:25). The last of the instructions in the passage is addressed to the whole company and is a call to consistency: since we live in the Spirit, let us walk in him. “Let us not be desirous of vain glory, provoking one another, envying one another” (Galatians 5:26). The final verse returns to the quarrel of verse 15 and to Romans 14’s danger of despising and judging. It names two of the ways liberty goes wrong between believers: provoking and envying."),
("sub", "11.4 What the passage does and does not give"),
("p", "The passage gives a command (walk in the Spirit), a conflict (flesh against Spirit), a list of forbidden works with a warning, and a list of fruit with a note that no law stands against it. It does not give a rule for meats, days or any other open matter. A reader who comes to Galatians 5 looking for the answer to whether a believer may eat meat offered to idols, or keep a day, or drink wine, will not find it. Paul gives the answer to those questions in Romans 14 and 1 Corinthians 8 to 10. In Galatians he gives the believer the Spirit and love and a list of what the flesh does, and he does not turn them into a decision procedure."),
("sub", "11.5 Two fixed edges and one open centre"),
("p", "The passages of the volume can be seen as having two fixed edges and an open centre. One edge is commanded: the works of the flesh, fornication, idolatry. The other edge is the yoke, which Paul refuses in Galatians 5:1 and Colossians 2. Between them lies a ground in which Paul says, “let every man be fully persuaded in his own mind,” and gives limits for the sake of the brother and the Lord. The volume does not draw the edges for the reader beyond what Paul draws. It says only that Paul draws them, and that he draws them in different letters for different cases."),
("ctbl", "Galatians 5:16–26 by the five questions.", [
    ("Question", "Answer from the passage", "Verse"),
    ("Does Paul command?", "Yes: walk in the Spirit; the works of the flesh are named with a warning", "5:16, 19–21"),
    ("What limit?", "No liberty to do the works of the flesh; no provoking or envying", "5:19–21, 26"),
    ("To whom is it owed?", "To the Spirit, and to one another", "5:16, 25, 26"),
    ("What ground?", "The flesh and the Spirit are contrary; they that are Christ’s have crucified the flesh", "5:17, 24"),
    ("What is unsaid?", "A rule for open matters; whose kingdom is meant at 5:21; the walk in detail", "Chapter 16"),
  ], [0.26, 0.52, 0.22]),
("sub", "11.6 What the volume takes from the passage"),
("bul", [
    "The works of the flesh are named with a warning, and no liberty is given there.",
    "The fruit of the Spirit is a list of dispositions, and no law stands against them.",
    "The passage gives no rule for open matters, and the volume builds none from it.",
    "The walk in the Spirit is read in Study 44 and the Law’s jurisdiction in Study 59.",
  ]),
]
